# Line-ending normalization.
#
# .editorconfig declares `end_of_line = lf` for `[*]`, but without this file
# that is only a formatting preference: on Windows, Git's default
# core.autocrlf=true checks every text file out as CRLF. `dotnet format
# CentralLogin.sln --verify-no-changes` (the Cake `FormatCheck` target) then
# fails with thousands of ENDOFLINE errors on a pristine clone, before a
# single line of contributor code is involved.
#
# Baseline: let Git detect text vs binary, normalize to LF in the repository,
# and check text files out as LF on every platform. `eol=lf` on the baseline
# is deliberate -- `* text=auto` alone would leave the checkout at the mercy
# of each developer's core.autocrlf. This mirrors `.editorconfig`'s `[*]`
# scope, so file types added later (.razor, .ts, ...) are covered by default.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts, which their tooling expects as CRLF. None are
# currently committed; the rules are here so that adding one does the right
# thing.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Vendored from Microsoft and Authenticode-signed: the signature only
# verifies over the CRLF bytes that were signed. The blob is stored as LF
# like everything else (so this adds no repository churn), but it must be
# checked out as CRLF or `Get-AuthenticodeSignature` reports NotSigned
# instead of Valid -- which is what core.autocrlf happened to give us before
# this file existed. Not part of the solution, so FormatCheck is unaffected.
# Any future signed .ps1 that is actually executed needs the same treatment.
dotnet-install.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Known binary types: never subject them to text detection or EOL rewriting.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.snk binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
